      "comment": "Process pixel chroma data using MMX instructions for multimedia optimization\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize source buffer end pointer based on pixel count (multiply by 4 for 32-bit pixels)\n2. Enter main processing loop while source pointer < end pointer\n3. Extract chroma U/V components from 16-bit buffer values using bit shifts\n   - Extract low byte (bits 0-7) and high byte (bits 8-15) separately\n   - Advance chroma buffer pointers by 2 bytes (1 16-bit value)\n4. Read 32-bit destination pixel data from buffer\n5. Extract ARGB components from destination pixel using bit shifts\n   - Alpha: bits 24-31, Green: bits 16-23, Blue: bits 8-15, Red: bits 0-7\n6. Pack chroma and pixel components into 64-bit values using MMX CONCAT operations\n7. Store packed data to both source buffer end and current source buffer positions\n8. Advance destination buffer pointer by 4 bytes and source buffer by 16 bytes\n9. Repeat loop until all pixels processed\n10. Return when processing complete\n\nParameters:\nnPixelCount (int): Number of pixels to process in this batch\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value, modifies global buffer pointers directly\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x4 - Size of 32-bit pixel data in bytes\n0x2 - Size of 16-bit chroma component in bytes  \n0x8 - Bit shift to extract high byte from 16-bit value\n0x10 - Bit shift to extract green component from 32-bit pixel\n0x18 - Bit shift to extract alpha component from 32-bit pixel\n\nState Machine:\nInitial: Load global buffer pointers to registers\nProcessing: Extract components, pack data, store results, advance pointers\nCompletion: Save updated buffer pointers back to globals, restore registers\n\nSpecial Cases:\nFunction uses MMX instruction set for efficient byte packing operations\nGlobal buffer pointers are preserved across function calls\nNo bounds checking performed - caller must ensure valid pixel count",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
