      "name": "ProcessPixelBuffersSIMD",
      "signature": "void ProcessPixelBuffersSIMD(int nPixelCount)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Performs high-performance SIMD pixel processing on dual buffer chains using MMX instructions.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate buffer endpoint addresses based on pixel count parameter (nPixelCount * 6 DWORDs per pixel)\n2. Process first buffer chain in parallel processing loop:\n   a. Load 32-bit pixel data from destination buffer, advance pointer\n   b. Convert pixel to MMX packed format with zero extension\n   c. Subtract baseline using saturated subtraction (psubusw) with g_qwMmxZeroMask\n   d. Bit-shift left by 2 for precision scaling (psllw)\n   e. Apply high multiplication with g_qwMmxMultiplier for gamma correction\n   f. Add rounding constant g_qwMmxRoundingConstant for proper quantization\n   g. Subtract rounding constant to finalize transformation\n   h. Extract and interleave components using packed word operations\n   i. Multiply by multiplier mask g_qwMmxMultiplierMask for final scaling\n   j. Store 6 transformed DWORDs to output buffer with proper bit alignment\n3. Update global buffer pointers for first chain\n4. Process second buffer chain with identical transformation algorithm\n5. Update global buffer pointers for second chain\n\nParameters:\nnPixelCount - Number of pixels to process (int)\n             Each pixel generates 6 DWORD outputs (24 bytes total)\n\nReturns:\nvoid\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Function processes exactly nPixelCount pixels from each buffer chain\n- Each input pixel (32-bit) generates 6 output DWORDs (192 bits)\n- Uses saturated arithmetic to prevent overflow during transformations\n- MMX state is preserved across function calls\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x3 (3) - Pixel stride multiplier for 6 DWORD output per pixel\n0x4 (4) - DWORD increment for source buffer advance\n0x18 (24) - Output buffer advance (6 DWORDs * 4 bytes)\n0x2 (2) - Precision bit shift for scaling\n0x8 (8) - Right shift for packed data extraction\n0x20 (32) - Right shift for high DWORD extraction\n0x28 (40) - Right shift for highest bits extraction\n0x30 (48) - Right shift for top word extraction\n\nError Handling:\n- No explicit error checking (optimized for performance)\n- Assumes valid global buffer pointers\n- Depends on caller to ensure sufficient buffer space\n\nMMX Operations:\n- PSUBUSW: Packed subtract unsigned with saturation (word)\n- PSLLW: Packed shift left logical (word)  \n- PMULHW: Packed multiply high (word)\n- PADDSW: Packed add signed with saturation (word)\n- PUNPCKLWD: Packed unpack low words\n- PUNPCKHWD: Packed unpack high words\n- MOVQ/MOVD: MMX data movement operations\n- POR: Packed bitwise OR\n\nGlobal Buffer Management:\nUses four global buffer pointers for dual-chain processing:\n- g_pDstBufferCurrent/g_pDstBufferEnd: Source pixel data chain 1\n- g_pSrcBufferCurrent/g_pSrcBufferEnd: Output pixel data chain 1\n- Buffer pointers automatically updated during processing",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
