      "name": "ProcessChromaSubsamplingMmx",
      "signature": "int ProcessChromaSubsamplingMmx(int nWidth)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "int",
      "comment": "Optimized MMX chroma subsampling processor for YUV to packed pixel conversion\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate end buffer pointer based on nWidth parameter for first buffer processing\n2. Load chroma buffer pointers (U and V channels) from global variables  \n3. First processing loop - interleave chroma with luminance data:\n   - Load 32-bit chroma U and V values using MOVD from buffer pointers\n   - Load 64-bit destination pixel data using MOVQ from current destination\n   - Unpack bytes using PUNPCKLBW to interleave U/V with luminance\n   - Store 64-bit result to source buffer using MOVQ operations\n   - Advance all buffer pointers and check loop boundary\n4. Update global buffer pointers after first loop completion\n5. Calculate end buffer pointer for second buffer processing  \n6. Second processing loop - identical MMX operations on second buffer set:\n   - Process g_pSrcBufferEnd, g_pDstBufferEnd, g_pChromaBufferU/V\n   - Same MMX interleaving operations with PUNPCKLBW/PUNPCKHBW\n   - Store results and advance pointers\n7. Restore global chroma buffer pointers after processing\n8. Return preserved EAX value (status/result from caller)\n\nParameters:\nnWidth - Width parameter used to calculate buffer processing boundaries\n         Multiplied by 2 and offset by -4 to determine loop end condition\n         Controls how many pixel pairs are processed in each loop\n\nReturns:\nValue preserved from input EAX register (typically processing status)\nZero indicates successful completion\nNon-zero may indicate error condition from calling function\n\nSpecial Cases:\nBuffer boundary calculations use (nWidth * 2 - 4) to account for:\n- 2x factor: Processing pixel pairs in 64-bit operations\n- -4 offset: Prevents buffer overflow on final iteration\nEmpty width (nWidth <= 2) results in no processing\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x8  - 8-byte stride for 64-bit destination buffer advances\n0x10 - 16-byte stride for source buffer advances (two 64-bit stores)\n0x4  - 4-byte stride for 32-bit chroma buffer advances\n0x18, 0x20, 0x28, 0x30, 0x38 - Bit shift offsets for byte extraction\n0x8, 0x10 - Bit shift offsets for chroma channel extraction\n\nError Handling:\nFunction preserves EAX register throughout execution\nNo internal error checking - assumes valid buffer pointers\nCaller responsible for buffer allocation and validity\nMMX state preserved using standard x86 calling convention\n\nMMX Operations:\nMOVD - Load 32-bit values into MMX registers\nMOVQ - Load/store 64-bit values to/from MMX registers  \nPUNPCKLBW - Unpack and interleave low bytes between registers\nPUNPCKHBW - Unpack and interleave high bytes between registers\nOperations convert separate Y/U/V planes to interleaved pixel format",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
      "method": "NOP",
